1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Locked inside this rubble are gems of incomparable beauty and value.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:11,000
They're the world's oldest substance and are made almost entirely of carbon.

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:18,000
Diamonds, transparent stones that come from the center of the earth, the sea, and the sky.

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Now, Diamond Mines on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:46,457 --> 00:00:50,880
From above, it looks like the landing site of an enormous UFO.

6
00:00:52,160 --> 00:00:57,840
But in reality, this is one of the most prolific open-pit diamond mines in the world.

7
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,000
In a remote part of Botswana, deep in the Kalahari Desert,

8
00:01:04,001 --> 00:01:09,000
the De Beers Company and the government of Botswana are digging for gem-quality diamonds.

9
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Last year, this mine, the Jwanang Mine, extracted over 11 million carats of diamonds,

10
00:01:18,001 --> 00:01:21,000
from 9 million tons of treated ore.

11
00:01:21,001 --> 00:01:23,000
Jwanang Mine, obviously, is one of the richer mines,

12
00:01:23,001 --> 00:01:26,000
or the richest mines in the world at this point in time.

13
00:01:26,001 --> 00:01:31,000
What you do is you dig down to a certain level.

14
00:01:31,001 --> 00:01:35,000
So you're digging down into that volcanic cone, if you like.

15
00:01:35,001 --> 00:01:40,000
And you need to get to the kimberlitic part of it, the kimberlite ore.

16
00:01:40,001 --> 00:01:43,000
That contains the diamonds. So you're going in at these angles.

17
00:01:43,001 --> 00:01:49,000
Now, in order that the walls, if you like, of this open pit mine don't fall in themselves,

18
00:01:49,001 --> 00:01:53,000
you stagger it down in a series of terraces.

19
00:01:53,001 --> 00:01:56,800
Terraces, like something you'd see in the Roman Colosseum,

20
00:01:56,900 --> 00:01:59,640
ring the interior of the pit.

21
00:01:59,740 --> 00:02:04,240
Mining is done on these benches, as well as on the mine's floor.

22
00:02:04,340 --> 00:02:08,140
At the base of the pit, ore is removed by hydraulic shovels

23
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:13,560
and loaded onto 170-ton capacity trucks for transport.

24
00:02:13,660 --> 00:02:16,560
Waste material is trucked to dumps.

25
00:02:16,660 --> 00:02:20,660
Diamond-bearing ore is transported to a crusher.

26
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:27,880
Experts say that 250 tons of rock are mined for every diamond that's recovered.

27
00:02:28,740 --> 00:02:30,740
Once the open pit is exhausted of diamonds,

28
00:02:33,560 --> 00:02:38,120
explosives are set to break up the hard rock, and miners go underground.

29
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:42,660
In bedrock adjacent to the funnel where the diamonds are,

30
00:02:43,300 --> 00:02:45,560
miners sink a vertical opening called a shaft.

31
00:02:47,380 --> 00:02:50,500
Horizontal tunnels join the main shaft to the working areas.

32
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:57,600
Drilling takes place toward the diamond bearing ore.

33
00:02:57,700 --> 00:03:01,800
Rock between the tunnels is blasted,

34
00:03:01,900 --> 00:03:05,740
removing the support from the overlying ore so that it caves

35
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:07,740
in.

36
00:03:07,840 --> 00:03:09,620
Now the pieces of rock are small enough

37
00:03:09,720 --> 00:03:14,120
to be removed from the troughs at the production level.

38
00:03:14,220 --> 00:03:16,520
Here, the rock is broken and transported

39
00:03:16,620 --> 00:03:19,520
to underground crushers before being carried to the surface.

40
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:26,240
The conveyor belts, which were first used in the 1920s,

41
00:03:26,241 --> 00:03:30,760
move the ore out of the mine. The first stop is the main

42
00:03:30,761 --> 00:03:33,300
treatment plant, where the crushed ore is washed and

43
00:03:33,301 --> 00:03:36,400
screened. Next, the concentrate moves

44
00:03:36,401 --> 00:03:38,840
onto a building called the Aquarium.

45
00:03:38,841 --> 00:03:41,200
This three-tower building consists of a floor for

46
00:03:41,201 --> 00:03:45,100
sorting, a floor for recovery of diamonds, and a bulk acid

47
00:03:45,101 --> 00:03:47,940
holding plant. From this point, the ore moves

48
00:03:47,941 --> 00:03:52,060
around in sealed containers. The rule here is what can't be

49
00:03:52,061 --> 00:03:54,060
can't be stolen.

50
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:58,000
Our first principle was to actually separate people,

51
00:03:58,100 --> 00:04:01,300
a physical separation of people actually

52
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:05,000
from where there was a high concentrate of diamonds.

53
00:04:05,100 --> 00:04:07,700
Our process is actually hands off.

54
00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:12,320
Automation has actually ensured that that happens.

55
00:04:12,420 --> 00:04:14,820
Using high technology equipment,

56
00:04:14,920 --> 00:04:19,820
rough diamonds are automatically sorted, cleaned, sized, weighed,

57
00:04:19,821 --> 00:04:23,820
packaged. Hidden surveillance cameras monitor the entire

58
00:04:23,821 --> 00:04:28,820
operation. In this control room, men track

59
00:04:28,821 --> 00:04:32,820
the flow of material by computer and watch for a security breach.

60
00:04:32,821 --> 00:04:35,820
They can see which doors have been opened, at what time, and

61
00:04:35,821 --> 00:04:38,820
for how long. Employees are photographed each

62
00:04:38,821 --> 00:04:42,820
time they go through a secure area. In high-risk areas where

63
00:04:42,821 --> 00:04:48,820
a diamond might be swallowed, they're also x-rayed.

64
00:04:48,821 --> 00:04:50,820
Once the diamonds are fully recovered,

65
00:04:50,920 --> 00:04:53,460
they travel by overhead conveyor through the plant

66
00:04:53,560 --> 00:04:55,820
and into an area so protected, it's

67
00:04:55,920 --> 00:04:57,320
off limits to film crews.

68
00:05:00,560 --> 00:05:02,560
Why all this fuss over an element that

69
00:05:02,660 --> 00:05:07,180
is totally carbon, just like the graphite you find in a pencil?

70
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:09,580
Because diamonds have been treasured by mankind

71
00:05:09,680 --> 00:05:12,580
for over 3,000 years.

72
00:05:12,680 --> 00:05:15,420
And because $7 billion worth of raw diamonds

73
00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:18,580
are sold every year, that translates

74
00:05:18,581 --> 00:05:22,420
into $50 billion worth of jewelry.

75
00:05:22,520 --> 00:05:25,120
But of the millions of diamonds purchased each year,

76
00:05:25,220 --> 00:05:27,580
few new owners know their rock's true history.

77
00:05:31,520 --> 00:05:34,320
Diamonds have to form at extremely high temperature

78
00:05:34,420 --> 00:05:35,840
and extremely high pressure.

79
00:05:35,940 --> 00:05:37,740
And the only place on Earth you find this

80
00:05:37,840 --> 00:05:40,740
is deep underground, perhaps 100 miles

81
00:05:40,840 --> 00:05:42,540
below the surface of the Earth.

82
00:05:42,640 --> 00:05:45,740
And there, carbon atoms slowly come together.

83
00:05:45,840 --> 00:05:47,780
They form larger and larger crystals.

84
00:05:47,781 --> 00:05:49,240
We know that some of those crystals

85
00:05:49,340 --> 00:05:52,820
may be the size of a soccer ball or maybe a watermelon,

86
00:05:52,920 --> 00:05:54,520
even.

87
00:05:54,620 --> 00:05:57,220
Over billions of years, at intense heat

88
00:05:57,320 --> 00:05:59,720
and unimaginable pressure, diamonds

89
00:05:59,820 --> 00:06:01,920
crystallize miles underground.

90
00:06:02,020 --> 00:06:03,520
They hitch a ride to the Earth's surface

91
00:06:03,620 --> 00:06:07,940
inside a carrot-shaped funnel called a kimberlite pipe.

92
00:06:08,040 --> 00:06:11,540
Kimberlite pipes contain minerals, including diamonds.

93
00:06:11,640 --> 00:06:14,880
Small volcanic explosions hoist the pipe to Earth's surface

94
00:06:14,980 --> 00:06:17,140
at Mach 1 speed.

95
00:06:17,141 --> 00:06:20,380
The famous pipe in Kimberley, South Africa, discovered in the

96
00:06:20,480 --> 00:06:28,380
late 1800s, was 450,000 square feet across and 3,500 feet deep.

97
00:06:28,480 --> 00:06:31,380
Our knowledge of diamonds was acquired slowly.

98
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:36,800
In 1772, French chemist Antoine Lavoisier shattered the myth

99
00:06:36,900 --> 00:06:38,800
that diamonds couldn't burn.

100
00:06:38,900 --> 00:06:42,800
He focused the sun's rays through a big circular lens and trained

101
00:06:42,900 --> 00:06:47,140
it on a small diamond sitting in a glass bowl filled with oxygen.

102
00:06:48,140 --> 00:06:50,840
And lo and behold, after a couple of minutes, the thing began smoking.

103
00:06:51,440 --> 00:06:52,680
Then it began smoldering.

104
00:06:52,860 --> 00:06:57,940
And then after a while, it was just a little pile of cinders, ashes to ashes, just like people.

105
00:06:57,941 --> 00:07:04,720
He discovered that diamond converted to carbon dioxide, and yet he never made that next step,

106
00:07:04,820 --> 00:07:08,780
the step that would suggest that carbon was what diamond is made of,

107
00:07:08,840 --> 00:07:10,980
that diamond is simply a pure form of carbon.

108
00:07:10,981 --> 00:07:17,460
The discovery of what diamonds are composed of fell to British chemist Smithson Tennant

109
00:07:17,461 --> 00:07:18,860
in the early 1800s.

110
00:07:19,420 --> 00:07:25,400
He took a very carefully weighed amount of diamond on the one hand, and then took the

111
00:07:25,401 --> 00:07:31,420
same weight of graphite or of charcoal on the other hand, and burned each of those in

112
00:07:31,421 --> 00:07:35,960
the way that Lavoisier had done, and showed that in each case you produce exactly the

113
00:07:35,961 --> 00:07:37,660
same amount of carbon dioxide.

114
00:07:37,661 --> 00:07:43,920
That proved beyond any doubt that both diamond and this black graphite,

115
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,900
the soft material of pencil lead, were chemically identical,

116
00:07:48,360 --> 00:07:50,140
even though they looked so completely different.

117
00:07:51,400 --> 00:07:54,660
The birth date of a diamond remained a mystery until the 1880s,

118
00:07:55,320 --> 00:07:58,500
because carbon, beyond a certain age, can't be dated.

119
00:07:59,620 --> 00:08:04,360
Then, scientists learned to date trace minerals found inside the ancient stone.

120
00:08:04,361 --> 00:08:08,700
It was discovered that diamonds were actually often much older

121
00:08:08,701 --> 00:08:10,440
than the kimberlite which they were found.

122
00:08:11,740 --> 00:08:13,800
Kimberlite is just the host of diamonds,

123
00:08:14,340 --> 00:08:16,860
the vehicle to get diamonds to the surface of the Earth.

124
00:08:17,440 --> 00:08:21,800
At 3.3 billion years of age, diamonds are almost as old as Earth itself.

125
00:08:22,980 --> 00:08:26,980
The first diamond miners in India in the 4th century B.C.

126
00:08:26,981 --> 00:08:29,240
only saw a glittering curiosity.

127
00:08:30,480 --> 00:08:33,300
Miners digging in the Krishna River gravel of southern India

128
00:08:33,301 --> 00:08:37,100
diamonds grew in the sand. After the rainy season, people

129
00:08:37,101 --> 00:08:40,980
would just flock to the riverbanks and begin sifting sand or whatever

130
00:08:40,981 --> 00:08:45,540
kind of material was there for diamonds. Rough diamonds, which

131
00:08:45,541 --> 00:08:48,500
looked like pieces of dirty glass, were thought to have magical

132
00:08:48,501 --> 00:08:51,540
properties. These ran the gamut from

133
00:08:51,541 --> 00:08:57,460
bringing dead animals back to life, to telling whether your

134
00:08:57,461 --> 00:09:01,700
mate was committing adultery, to making the wary invisible, to

135
00:09:01,701 --> 00:09:05,700
to giving you courage, to putting out fires,

136
00:09:05,701 --> 00:09:08,700
anything you could imagine. India's diamond production

137
00:09:08,701 --> 00:09:12,700
reached its zenith in the 16th century with a maximum output

138
00:09:12,701 --> 00:09:16,700
of between 50,000 and 100,000 carats.

139
00:09:16,701 --> 00:09:19,700
But that volume was almost eclipsed by the discovery of

140
00:09:19,701 --> 00:09:25,700
great diamond deposits in Brazil in 1725 on the Rio dos Marinos

141
00:09:25,701 --> 00:09:29,700
River. Using a wooden washing pan,

142
00:09:29,701 --> 00:09:32,340
fine sand and clay was flushed away,

143
00:09:32,440 --> 00:09:34,440
and the diamonds were picked out by hand

144
00:09:34,540 --> 00:09:38,100
from the remaining heavy minerals.

145
00:09:38,200 --> 00:09:42,440
Between 1730 and 1822, it's estimated that Brazil

146
00:09:42,540 --> 00:09:46,160
produced almost 3 million carats of diamonds.

147
00:09:46,260 --> 00:09:49,660
Neither Brazil nor India would come close to the flood of

148
00:09:49,760 --> 00:09:54,060
diamonds found in another remote part of the world.

149
00:09:55,960 --> 00:09:59,220
In 1866, the great South African diamond

150
00:09:59,221 --> 00:10:02,120
African diamond rush made world headlines.

151
00:10:02,121 --> 00:10:06,260
It began when 15-year-old Aramis Jacobs spotted a transparent

152
00:10:06,261 --> 00:10:09,360
stone on his father's farm on the south bank of the Orange

153
00:10:09,361 --> 00:10:11,800
River.

154
00:10:11,801 --> 00:10:16,540
That stone was a 21-carat diamond.

155
00:10:16,541 --> 00:10:19,180
People just rushed to South Africa.

156
00:10:19,181 --> 00:10:21,700
And at that time, transportation was not very good.

157
00:10:21,701 --> 00:10:25,080
So it took them about three or four months to get there.

158
00:10:25,081 --> 00:10:28,280
Within a year, a feverish mix of American veterans

159
00:10:28,281 --> 00:10:34,000
the Civil War, Eastern and Western Europeans and Canadians swarmed the Cape of Good Hope

160
00:10:34,001 --> 00:10:36,420
to mine diamonds. The black diggers were basically

161
00:10:36,421 --> 00:10:38,898
hired labor and they were sent in to do the dirty work.

162
00:10:38,899 --> 00:10:40,340
And it's not that the white guys

163
00:10:40,341 --> 00:10:42,320
didn't do the job too.

164
00:10:42,321 --> 00:10:48,260
Everybody was out there with the flies and the sun and the sewage.

165
00:10:48,261 --> 00:10:52,000
Diamonds were hand washed from the alluvial or riverbed pebbles.

166
00:10:52,001 --> 00:10:55,660
What happened after that was people began fanning out into the backcountry, away from

167
00:10:55,661 --> 00:10:58,300
in hopes that they might find diamonds there.

168
00:10:58,400 --> 00:11:00,700
And indeed, they did.

169
00:11:00,800 --> 00:11:04,660
In 1869, the practice of dry digging for diamonds

170
00:11:04,760 --> 00:11:07,400
was born.

171
00:11:07,500 --> 00:11:10,700
These images were filmed by Thomas Edison in Kimberly, South

172
00:11:10,800 --> 00:11:13,920
Africa in 1917. The deeper they dug,

173
00:11:14,020 --> 00:11:17,520
the more that it appeared that the diamonds had come from below,

174
00:11:17,620 --> 00:11:20,760
not been washed from above, and that perhaps this was the mother

175
00:11:20,860 --> 00:11:24,960
rock. 20 miles north lay a farm

176
00:11:24,961 --> 00:11:28,800
by Dutch settlers named De Beers.

177
00:11:28,900 --> 00:11:32,860
On their property, miners found large rough diamonds like nothing

178
00:11:32,960 --> 00:11:36,000
they'd seen before.

179
00:11:36,100 --> 00:11:39,540
Thomas Edison captured on film the area miners named

180
00:11:39,640 --> 00:11:40,640
Kimberly.

181
00:11:40,740 --> 00:11:42,640
It was a boom town.

182
00:11:42,740 --> 00:11:45,120
A 17-year-old Englishman of delicate health,

183
00:11:45,220 --> 00:11:48,620
but with a wealth of ambition, joined his older brother at the

184
00:11:48,720 --> 00:11:49,960
claim in Kimberly.

185
00:11:50,060 --> 00:11:51,720
The boy's name was Cecil Rhodes.

186
00:11:54,960 --> 00:11:59,640
I think people will remember Cecil Rhodes as two people, really.

187
00:12:01,060 --> 00:12:03,660
One, he was a great adventurer.

188
00:12:04,420 --> 00:12:10,800
He certainly was very active in expanding the territories of the British Empire at that time.

189
00:12:11,280 --> 00:12:15,880
But also he'll be remembered as being a great entrepreneur.

190
00:12:16,600 --> 00:12:19,300
And a man keen on not becoming a miner.

191
00:12:20,020 --> 00:12:21,240
It was extremely dangerous.

192
00:12:21,241 --> 00:12:22,720
Many people were killed.

193
00:12:22,721 --> 00:12:30,720
And there are reports of huge piles of rock rumbling down the hillside and burying miners.

194
00:12:30,721 --> 00:12:35,720
Sometimes the neighbor would find his predecessor's body somewhere in a shaft.

195
00:12:35,721 --> 00:12:39,480
There were hundreds, perhaps thousands, of individual diggers there.

196
00:12:39,481 --> 00:12:41,480
It was a very chaotic scene.

197
00:12:41,481 --> 00:12:45,720
Imagine, if you will, something like a gold rush kind of era.

198
00:12:45,721 --> 00:12:49,600
It was a real wild west town.

199
00:12:49,601 --> 00:12:51,900
The only thing that could have made it more dangerous

200
00:12:51,901 --> 00:12:56,900
was the use of dynamite, which would come into use in 1900.

201
00:12:56,901 --> 00:13:00,940
The land was divided into 31-foot square prospects.

202
00:13:00,941 --> 00:13:03,640
Roadways separated the claims.

203
00:13:03,641 --> 00:13:07,260
It is said to be the largest hand excavation in human history.

204
00:13:07,261 --> 00:13:11,760
The works of ancient Babylonia and Egypt not accepted.

205
00:13:11,761 --> 00:13:14,560
With thousands of feet honeycombed with pockets,

206
00:13:14,561 --> 00:13:17,900
the yellow earth of Kimberley began to give way.

207
00:13:17,901 --> 00:13:19,160
Everybody was getting flooded out.

208
00:13:19,161 --> 00:13:23,360
Cecil Rhodes got a hold of the only pump for about 600 miles around and

209
00:13:23,361 --> 00:13:24,760
rented it out and made a fortune.

210
00:13:26,740 --> 00:13:29,620
Frequent cave-ins smothered hopes of easy riches.

211
00:13:30,260 --> 00:13:33,440
As miners gave up, they sold their claims to Cecil Rhodes.

212
00:13:34,040 --> 00:13:40,700
It was amongst this chaos that Cecil Rhodes saw that the best way of taking

213
00:13:40,701 --> 00:13:46,160
this industry forward and without it destroying itself there and then was for

214
00:13:46,161 --> 00:13:47,440
all the producers to come together.

215
00:13:49,160 --> 00:13:52,460
By the age of 27, Rhodes was the largest mine owner

216
00:13:52,560 --> 00:13:54,460
in Kimberley.

217
00:13:54,560 --> 00:13:57,160
As for the brothers De Beers, they sold their farm

218
00:13:57,260 --> 00:14:01,900
for a mere 6,000 pounds, leaving behind only their name.

219
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:04,900
Cecil Rhodes borrowed the De Beers name when he squeezed out

220
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:08,820
his only real competitor, Barney Barnato.

221
00:14:08,920 --> 00:14:11,220
The strategy used by both sides was quite different,

222
00:14:11,320 --> 00:14:15,120
because on the one hand, Barnato actually had more money

223
00:14:15,220 --> 00:14:17,860
than Rhodes at this time.

224
00:14:17,861 --> 00:14:20,620
Rhodes pushed for a partnership with Barnato.

225
00:14:20,621 --> 00:14:24,200
A financial battle ensued, and ultimately Rhodes won the day.

226
00:14:24,201 --> 00:14:27,420
He merged the holdings of both men into De Beers consolidated

227
00:14:27,421 --> 00:14:29,200
diamond mining.

228
00:14:29,201 --> 00:14:32,260
With Rhodes' subsequent purchase of the now-famous Deutzpahnen

229
00:14:32,261 --> 00:14:34,620
and Boulfontaine diamond pipe mines,

230
00:14:34,621 --> 00:14:38,120
Rhodes controlled more than 95% of the world's diamonds

231
00:14:38,121 --> 00:14:40,760
by the turn of the century.

232
00:14:40,761 --> 00:14:43,720
To ensure the present and future value of diamonds,

233
00:14:43,721 --> 00:14:46,760
Cecil Rhodes began a movement toward centralized control

234
00:14:46,761 --> 00:14:48,760
of their production and release,

235
00:14:48,761 --> 00:14:52,760
a move that had no precedent in world economic practice.

236
00:14:52,761 --> 00:14:55,760
He predicated the volume of diamonds he'd release for market

237
00:14:55,761 --> 00:14:58,760
on the number of marriages every year in the United States,

238
00:14:58,761 --> 00:15:02,760
for engagement rings were the bulk of the diamond gem market.

239
00:15:02,761 --> 00:15:05,760
Distribution of his diamonds would go through a single channel.

240
00:15:05,761 --> 00:15:09,760
The birth of what we came to know as single-channel marketing

241
00:15:09,761 --> 00:15:12,760
was really in 1890, a couple of years

242
00:15:12,761 --> 00:15:15,760
after the establishment of De Beers Consolidated.

243
00:15:15,761 --> 00:15:23,680
And this was when a group of Diamantez based here in Haddon Garden came together and created

244
00:15:23,681 --> 00:15:25,180
the London Diamond Syndicate.

245
00:15:25,660 --> 00:15:30,980
And this syndicate basically took the production from the De Beers mines and marketed them

246
00:15:30,981 --> 00:15:32,040
onto the world.

247
00:15:32,960 --> 00:15:37,860
The stage was set for an up-and-comer to reap the rewards of Cecil Rhoades' plan.

248
00:15:39,660 --> 00:15:44,280
Ernest Oppenheimer moved to London, worked for a company called Duncan Spooler with his

249
00:15:44,281 --> 00:15:44,981
uncle.

250
00:15:44,982 --> 00:15:50,920
and eventually in 1902, just shortly after the death of Cecil Rhodes, went down to Kimberley

251
00:15:50,921 --> 00:15:55,580
and began buying diamonds from De Beers to sell back through his uncle's firm.

252
00:15:56,580 --> 00:16:00,880
In Kimberley, Oppenheimer saw the gradual erosion of the diamond industry.

253
00:16:01,700 --> 00:16:06,900
Single-channel marketing worked well as long as this one area produced 95% of the world's diamonds.

254
00:16:07,780 --> 00:16:13,280
But new producers from Angola, the Congo, and Namibia were pumping diamonds onto the world market,

255
00:16:13,281 --> 00:16:16,060
which would ultimately force the price of diamonds down.

256
00:16:16,860 --> 00:16:19,580
With backing from financier J.P. Morgan,

257
00:16:20,520 --> 00:16:22,900
Oppenheimer formed the Anglo-American Corporation

258
00:16:22,901 --> 00:16:26,620
to exploit a gold field in Africa's East Whitwater End.

259
00:16:28,380 --> 00:16:31,140
In the meantime, the onset of World War I

260
00:16:31,141 --> 00:16:32,820
drove the diamond industry down.

261
00:16:33,500 --> 00:16:35,620
Money was spent on staples, not on diamonds.

262
00:16:36,660 --> 00:16:39,900
When Oppenheimer returned to South Africa in the 1920s,

263
00:16:39,901 --> 00:16:42,600
he was in a position to buy up some diamond claims

264
00:16:42,601 --> 00:16:46,360
along the west coast of Namibia.

265
00:16:46,361 --> 00:16:48,740
Later, he traded that mine for De Beers stock

266
00:16:48,741 --> 00:16:51,200
and seized control of the company.

267
00:16:51,201 --> 00:16:54,500
In 1926, he became a De Beers director.

268
00:16:54,501 --> 00:16:58,500
In 1929, he was its chairman.

269
00:16:58,501 --> 00:17:00,400
By then, he had taken the diamond business

270
00:17:00,401 --> 00:17:02,000
to its next level.

271
00:17:02,001 --> 00:17:04,940
He not only controlled the world's diamond supply,

272
00:17:04,941 --> 00:17:09,440
he controlled the key players who would sell it.

273
00:17:09,441 --> 00:17:12,100
This allowed him then to go on to create

274
00:17:12,101 --> 00:17:15,440
his vision of long-term stability within the industry.

275
00:17:15,900 --> 00:17:21,160
And this is when, in 1930, we see the bringing together again of all these producers now

276
00:17:21,161 --> 00:17:26,900
and the creation of what became known as the Central Selling Organization.

277
00:17:31,520 --> 00:17:36,940
The Oppenheimer family successfully ran De Beers as a cartel right into the 21st century,

278
00:17:37,420 --> 00:17:41,373
even though it meant they would be legally prevented from doing business on U.S.

279
00:17:41,374 --> 00:17:42,074
soil.

280
00:17:42,100 --> 00:17:48,340
Only recently has the diamond monopoly lost its vice-like grip.

281
00:17:48,440 --> 00:17:51,480
A proliferation of diamond mines outside of De Beers' control

282
00:17:51,580 --> 00:17:55,240
has, at last, begun to erode the giant's dominion over the

283
00:17:55,340 --> 00:17:56,940
world's most precious gem.

284
00:18:00,560 --> 00:18:03,360
The Oppenheimers had controlled the world's diamond supply

285
00:18:03,460 --> 00:18:06,860
for almost a century when communist Russia entered the

286
00:18:06,960 --> 00:18:09,460
arena in 1947.

287
00:18:09,461 --> 00:18:14,660
In 1954, the first kimberlite pipe was found in Siberia.

288
00:18:14,661 --> 00:18:20,320
In 1955, two more pipes were discovered within 10 days of each other.

289
00:18:21,320 --> 00:18:24,360
The Russian mine Akil is one of the richest on Earth.

290
00:18:25,140 --> 00:18:28,200
Not in terms of the volume of material that is produced,

291
00:18:28,740 --> 00:18:33,500
but because of the large percentage of gem-quality diamonds.

292
00:18:33,760 --> 00:18:37,340
These are perfect octahedra, beautifully clear stones.

293
00:18:37,341 --> 00:18:43,960
To reach the stones, one must go through ground that has been permanently frozen since the last ice age,

294
00:18:44,260 --> 00:18:48,780
which lasted from about 1.6 million years to 10,000 years ago.

295
00:18:49,620 --> 00:18:55,100
In these extreme arctic wilderness conditions, a spring day is minus 30 degrees.

296
00:18:55,460 --> 00:19:03,180
Mining in these areas posed an entirely new operational vista and an engineering challenge.

297
00:19:03,181 --> 00:19:08,180
And the reason was that once permafrost is opened up,

298
00:19:08,181 --> 00:19:12,180
and for example, when houses are built on the permafrost,

299
00:19:12,181 --> 00:19:16,180
the warmth from the house will tend to melt the ground under you,

300
00:19:16,181 --> 00:19:19,180
and the foundations will give way correspondingly.

301
00:19:19,181 --> 00:19:22,180
The task became keeping the ground permanently frozen,

302
00:19:22,181 --> 00:19:25,180
so that the foundations of what was built on the permafrost

303
00:19:25,181 --> 00:19:28,180
wouldn't sink and fall apart.

304
00:19:28,181 --> 00:19:32,180
When it came to the scale of a mine, the challenge was formidable.

305
00:19:32,181 --> 00:19:40,000
They wrapped the pylons essentially in a hot water blanket material and at enormous cost

306
00:19:40,001 --> 00:19:43,860
and essentially kept the pylons cold.

307
00:19:44,740 --> 00:19:46,380
And there were only two alternatives.

308
00:19:46,520 --> 00:19:49,700
You either keep them hot or you keep them cold, but you have to keep them permanently in that way.

309
00:19:51,760 --> 00:19:55,500
The Soviet Union threatened to flood the world's markets with their diamonds.

310
00:19:56,140 --> 00:19:58,440
De Beers moved swiftly and bought up their stockpile.

311
00:19:58,441 --> 00:20:01,000
That arrangement collapsed in 1991,

312
00:20:01,580 --> 00:20:03,560
along with the fall of the Soviet empire.

313
00:20:04,860 --> 00:20:08,040
While some portion of diamond production remained with De Beers,

314
00:20:08,380 --> 00:20:11,320
a percentage is now sold outside the De Beers cartel,

315
00:20:11,760 --> 00:20:15,060
weakening its position as the only true supplier of gem-quality diamonds.

316
00:20:18,400 --> 00:20:22,760
The latest entry into the multi-billion-dollar diamond industry is Canada.

317
00:20:23,580 --> 00:20:26,880
Back in the late 1970s, a British Columbian geologist

318
00:20:26,881 --> 00:20:31,120
taking samples in the tundra of Canada's Northwest Territories.

319
00:20:31,220 --> 00:20:33,520
Skeptics thought he was mad as a hatter.

320
00:20:33,620 --> 00:20:36,720
But Chuck Phippke was a true Indiana Jones.

321
00:20:36,820 --> 00:20:39,620
He followed kimberlite trails until he found their source

322
00:20:39,720 --> 00:20:41,320
at the bottom of a lake.

323
00:20:41,420 --> 00:20:46,040
In 1985, I was collecting a sample just right over there

324
00:20:46,140 --> 00:20:47,240
on that esker.

325
00:20:47,340 --> 00:20:54,240
And we took the sample back to the lab in Kelowna,

326
00:20:54,241 --> 00:20:58,640
and we found it had about 5,000 kimberlite pyros

327
00:20:58,740 --> 00:21:03,040
and 1,700 chrome diopsides, which are minerals that come out

328
00:21:03,140 --> 00:21:05,780
of diamond-bearing kimberlites.

329
00:21:05,880 --> 00:21:08,480
With money borrowed from working class friends,

330
00:21:08,580 --> 00:21:11,200
Thipke and fellow geologist Stu Blusson

331
00:21:11,300 --> 00:21:15,000
staked out an area just smaller than Rhode Island.

332
00:21:15,100 --> 00:21:18,100
After years of failure and often on the brink of bankruptcy,

333
00:21:18,200 --> 00:21:22,800
in 1991, the stubborn geologists struck it rich.

334
00:21:22,801 --> 00:21:26,880
Now in partnership with BHP Minerals, a huge mining company,

335
00:21:27,500 --> 00:21:31,800
the Canadian mine known as Ikati is providing close to 4% of the

336
00:21:31,801 --> 00:21:34,720
world's diamonds by weight and 5% by value.

337
00:21:35,460 --> 00:21:38,640
The gems coming out of Ikati are some of the highest quality gem

338
00:21:38,641 --> 00:21:42,620
diamonds in the world. These kimberlite pipes are under

339
00:21:42,621 --> 00:21:45,220
lakes. To prep them for mining, they're

340
00:21:45,221 --> 00:21:48,720
emptied of fish and then drained. Basically, you just dig a big hole

341
00:21:48,721 --> 00:21:52,460
and you dig a hole that's wider than the pipe, of course, because you have to go down and

342
00:21:52,461 --> 00:21:55,600
sort of, you have to have a roadway to go down into this thing.

343
00:21:56,760 --> 00:21:59,280
Using a steam shovel about three stories high,

344
00:21:59,800 --> 00:22:02,680
tons of diamond ore is scooped up from the former lake bed

345
00:22:02,681 --> 00:22:04,520
and dumped into a huge truck.

346
00:22:05,720 --> 00:22:08,680
At a nearby plant, the ore is run through a series of sieves

347
00:22:08,681 --> 00:22:11,180
and rollers and other equipment that crushes it up.

348
00:22:11,420 --> 00:22:12,840
They don't want to crush it up too much, of course,

349
00:22:12,960 --> 00:22:15,060
because they'll crush any diamonds in there.

350
00:22:15,240 --> 00:22:17,980
In a final step, the heavier diamondiferous material

351
00:22:17,981 --> 00:22:20,980
is put through a device which x-rays every little particle

352
00:22:20,981 --> 00:22:22,580
as it goes down a conveyor belt.

353
00:22:24,240 --> 00:22:26,080
The diamonds will fluoresce,

354
00:22:26,320 --> 00:22:27,900
but diamonds aren't all, they X-ray.

355
00:22:28,620 --> 00:22:30,920
They do everything from X-raying

356
00:22:30,921 --> 00:22:34,260
your laptop computer and your boots

357
00:22:34,261 --> 00:22:37,500
to putting you through a little airlock device

358
00:22:37,501 --> 00:22:39,720
which takes a look at your body

359
00:22:39,721 --> 00:22:42,600
just to make sure that you don't have any diamonds on you.

360
00:22:43,800 --> 00:22:46,180
Pipke and Blusson's early shoestring investors

361
00:22:46,181 --> 00:22:47,400
are now millionaires,

362
00:22:47,680 --> 00:22:49,800
and the geologists each have a net worth

363
00:22:49,801 --> 00:22:52,600
to half a billion dollars. They're still wearing the same

364
00:22:52,601 --> 00:22:55,800
ratty old shoes and pants. They're in some ways not touched

365
00:22:55,801 --> 00:22:59,400
at all. Where are diamonds found?

366
00:22:59,401 --> 00:23:02,300
An obvious answer is around a kimberlite pipe.

367
00:23:02,301 --> 00:23:05,500
But once that kimberlite host weathers, diamonds are found in

368
00:23:05,501 --> 00:23:10,300
riverbeds and the sea. In 1961, a Texas businessman

369
00:23:10,301 --> 00:23:13,300
explored shallow water sea mining adjacent to the coastal

370
00:23:13,301 --> 00:23:17,800
strip north of the Orange River, known today as Namibia.

371
00:23:17,801 --> 00:23:21,400
In 1967, De Beers took over from this early prospector

372
00:23:21,500 --> 00:23:23,740
and began taking samples.

373
00:23:23,840 --> 00:23:27,040
By 1970, they were able to sample sediments in water

374
00:23:27,140 --> 00:23:30,440
of up to 656 feet.

375
00:23:30,540 --> 00:23:32,740
Their efforts led to the discovery of a diamond

376
00:23:32,840 --> 00:23:35,760
deposit in the Atlantic Ocean on the Middle Continental

377
00:23:35,860 --> 00:23:37,460
Shelf.

378
00:23:37,560 --> 00:23:40,060
The diamonds had been carried to the Atlantic as the land's

379
00:23:40,160 --> 00:23:43,960
surface eroded over millions of years.

380
00:23:44,060 --> 00:23:47,500
Within 10 years, De Beers had recovered over 400,000

381
00:23:47,501 --> 00:23:52,722
carats of diamonds from the sea, mining at a maximum depth of 500 feet.

382
00:23:52,723 --> 00:23:54,120
The first challenge

383
00:23:54,121 --> 00:23:57,652
in deep sea mining is removing the gravel from the seabag.

384
00:23:57,653 --> 00:23:59,600
The gravel in which the diamonds

385
00:23:59,601 --> 00:24:07,120
are preserved or concentrated is literally sucked up as a vacuum cleaner would suck up

386
00:24:07,121 --> 00:24:09,580
material. De Beers' mining ships are converted

387
00:24:09,581 --> 00:24:12,526
oil rigs with a hole in the middle of the boat.

388
00:24:12,527 --> 00:24:15,660
And it was through that that the suction mechanism

389
00:24:15,661 --> 00:24:19,880
is put and then lowered onto the seabed,

390
00:24:20,460 --> 00:24:24,400
and the gravel is then brought up into that.

391
00:24:24,900 --> 00:24:28,360
That's partially concentrated, which is the great advantage of that ship.

392
00:24:28,900 --> 00:24:31,720
The initial concentration is done on board,

393
00:24:31,721 --> 00:24:33,720
the waste material is thrown overboard,

394
00:24:34,520 --> 00:24:37,520
and the ship progressively moves up and down.

395
00:24:38,220 --> 00:24:41,020
There are two methods to suck gravel from the sea.

396
00:24:41,780 --> 00:24:44,540
The horizontal system uses a seabed crawler.

397
00:24:45,400 --> 00:24:48,280
Gravel is brought onto the ship through flexible slurry hoses.

398
00:24:49,080 --> 00:24:53,080
The vertical system consists of a large diameter rotating drill bit.

399
00:24:53,780 --> 00:24:57,120
The system actually lifts the gravel from the seabed to the ship.

400
00:24:57,820 --> 00:25:01,800
It's possible to go back to the same primary holes,

401
00:25:02,140 --> 00:25:03,540
nori holes as they're called,

402
00:25:04,100 --> 00:25:08,240
in which are irregularities in the basement of the seafloor.

403
00:25:08,241 --> 00:25:13,460
And these are recharged after a big storm,

404
00:25:13,461 --> 00:25:15,260
and they recharge it with diamonds

405
00:25:15,360 --> 00:25:18,060
and so they can go back again periodically,

406
00:25:18,160 --> 00:25:23,600
suck the material up, and it will be diamond bearing again.

407
00:25:23,700 --> 00:25:26,500
On board, the gravel is separated into three sizes

408
00:25:26,600 --> 00:25:28,400
by a screening process.

409
00:25:28,500 --> 00:25:32,720
The smallest size is mostly waste quartz and is discarded.

410
00:25:32,820 --> 00:25:35,080
The largest size rarely yields diamonds

411
00:25:35,180 --> 00:25:37,520
and is washed overboard from the ship.

412
00:25:37,620 --> 00:25:40,660
The middle size is processed to separate the heavy minerals,

413
00:25:40,661 --> 00:25:43,900
including diamond from the light waste mineral.

414
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:47,060
The remaining concentrate passes through an x-ray machine,

415
00:25:47,160 --> 00:25:49,240
which further separates the fluorescent diamonds

416
00:25:49,340 --> 00:25:51,340
from the rest of the material.

417
00:25:51,440 --> 00:25:53,300
After the final sort, the diamonds

418
00:25:53,400 --> 00:25:54,980
are ready for transport.

419
00:25:55,080 --> 00:25:58,980
And then they're put on motorboats, motor launchers,

420
00:25:59,080 --> 00:26:01,980
and then taken ashore.

421
00:26:02,080 --> 00:26:04,260
From diamond mine to jewelry store,

422
00:26:04,360 --> 00:26:06,360
only the best stones will make the grade.

423
00:26:06,361 --> 00:26:09,700
trade.

424
00:26:09,701 --> 00:26:14,160
This is Gaborone, the capital city of Botswana.

425
00:26:14,161 --> 00:26:17,220
The prosperity of this region is due to the country's rich diamond

426
00:26:17,221 --> 00:26:19,800
deposits.

427
00:26:19,801 --> 00:26:22,480
Diamonds from Juenang Mine are sorted and valued

428
00:26:22,481 --> 00:26:23,920
in this building.

429
00:26:23,921 --> 00:26:25,600
What we're doing at this end of the floor

430
00:26:25,601 --> 00:26:29,720
is valuing large diamonds into one of many hundreds

431
00:26:29,721 --> 00:26:33,560
of classifications by color, quality, shape.

432
00:26:33,561 --> 00:26:36,100
Diamonds are first separated into gem quality,

433
00:26:36,101 --> 00:26:39,380
near gem quality, and industrial grade.

434
00:26:39,381 --> 00:26:41,780
The purpose of sorting is to estimate an asking price

435
00:26:41,781 --> 00:26:43,520
for the rough diamonds.

436
00:26:43,521 --> 00:26:46,680
A sorters' initial training lasts six months.

437
00:26:46,681 --> 00:26:49,580
If he's not maybe very good at sorting,

438
00:26:49,581 --> 00:26:54,040
we don't, that's when we just say to them, bye bye.

439
00:26:54,041 --> 00:26:56,620
Experienced sorters can make 16,000

440
00:26:56,621 --> 00:26:59,860
different classifications, like color, size, weight,

441
00:26:59,861 --> 00:27:04,420
model, clarity, grade, and every subcategory they're in.

442
00:27:04,421 --> 00:27:07,560
Small stones are sorted by computer.

443
00:27:07,561 --> 00:27:10,460
Human hand sorters used to face so-called run of mine

444
00:27:10,461 --> 00:27:13,480
diamonds, which included every classification.

445
00:27:13,481 --> 00:27:17,200
Whereas now, we have machines actually helping us

446
00:27:17,201 --> 00:27:19,800
to sort about 60%.

447
00:27:19,801 --> 00:27:21,360
Diamonds that pass through this room

448
00:27:21,361 --> 00:27:25,100
represent 70% of the gross domestic product of Botswana,

449
00:27:25,101 --> 00:27:27,720
a whopping $4 billion.

450
00:27:27,721 --> 00:27:33,920
So it's a huge earner dollar wise for Botswana

451
00:27:33,921 --> 00:27:37,020
economy, and it has transformed the economy over the last 20

452
00:27:37,120 --> 00:27:40,420
years. Sorted rough diamonds are

453
00:27:40,520 --> 00:27:44,360
offered for sale in London by De Beers 10 times a year.

454
00:27:44,460 --> 00:27:48,860
An elite 125 diamond jewelry manufacturers are invited to

455
00:27:48,960 --> 00:27:53,180
these events. You go to London, and they

456
00:27:53,280 --> 00:27:58,920
will show you the kind of goods that you are experienced to

457
00:27:59,020 --> 00:28:02,380
manufacture, and you can handle it, and you have the proper

458
00:28:02,381 --> 00:28:07,980
distribution, and they will award you a site accordingly.

459
00:28:07,981 --> 00:28:10,580
The De Beers site holders manufacture many of the most

460
00:28:10,581 --> 00:28:14,420
exquisite pieces of diamond jewelry on the market.

461
00:28:14,421 --> 00:28:18,820
These are compilations of years and years of housing and holding

462
00:28:18,821 --> 00:28:21,740
an inventory until you actually have enough stones, because what

463
00:28:21,741 --> 00:28:24,340
happens is that a lot of the diamonds have to match each

464
00:28:24,341 --> 00:28:27,440
other. This fancy yellow diamond

465
00:28:27,441 --> 00:28:29,780
necklace took three years to assemble.

466
00:28:29,781 --> 00:28:32,100
Its yellow color comes from traces of nitrogen.

467
00:28:32,780 --> 00:28:34,280
The color increases its value.

468
00:28:34,920 --> 00:28:39,920
It wholesales for $1.2 million and retails for $2.4 million.

469
00:28:40,420 --> 00:28:45,320
There's a lot of royalty around today with the Arab world

470
00:28:45,321 --> 00:28:48,700
and some of the other countries, the emerging countries.

471
00:28:49,540 --> 00:28:53,700
There are individuals who are in a position to buy these.

472
00:28:54,240 --> 00:28:56,980
Stones are matched by color, clarity, and shape.

473
00:28:56,981 --> 00:29:00,500
The piece here in the middle is a tiara as well as a necklace.

474
00:29:00,780 --> 00:29:01,820
It's shown here as a tiara.

475
00:29:03,260 --> 00:29:06,420
It's got pear shapes and rounds and some marquees

476
00:29:06,421 --> 00:29:09,180
and some smaller pear shapes, yellow pear shapes, white pear shapes.

477
00:29:10,460 --> 00:29:14,280
Most of the stones in this tiara are graded D, flawless.

478
00:29:14,860 --> 00:29:15,740
These are...

479
00:29:15,741 --> 00:29:17,040
These are signature...

480
00:29:17,041 --> 00:29:17,820
These are like paintings.

481
00:29:18,000 --> 00:29:19,900
Yeah, these are signature pieces for us, though.

482
00:29:20,340 --> 00:29:23,900
People will recognize that there's only a few select companies in the world

483
00:29:23,901 --> 00:29:26,360
that can assemble something like that.

484
00:29:26,980 --> 00:29:30,480
The metamorphosis from rough cut diamond to gemstone begins

485
00:29:30,580 --> 00:29:33,220
in the expert hands of the diamond cleaver.

486
00:29:33,320 --> 00:29:36,220
Each diamond is cut to maximize the weight of the stone

487
00:29:36,320 --> 00:29:39,320
while still ridding it of flaws.

488
00:29:39,420 --> 00:29:43,660
Smaller diamonds of lesser value are often cut by laser.

489
00:29:43,760 --> 00:29:46,760
So if you see something inside of the stone that's not

490
00:29:46,860 --> 00:29:48,600
going to look good in the main stone,

491
00:29:48,700 --> 00:29:53,340
somehow you want to saw that off or cleave it off

492
00:29:53,440 --> 00:29:55,280
and make two stones.

493
00:29:55,281 --> 00:29:59,780
But always you're trying to look for the biggest, the best stone.

494
00:30:00,160 --> 00:30:04,120
You've got to let the stone tell you what it wants to be made into.

495
00:30:04,880 --> 00:30:08,440
Diamond cutter is apprentice alongside a master for five years.

496
00:30:09,000 --> 00:30:10,560
Harvey Lieberman trained with his father.

497
00:30:11,040 --> 00:30:13,180
I'm going to take this rough stone.

498
00:30:13,360 --> 00:30:15,220
It's approximately eight carats.

499
00:30:15,760 --> 00:30:18,440
Nothing's been done to it as of yet.

500
00:30:19,480 --> 00:30:23,000
And what I'm going to do is make an initial facet

501
00:30:23,001 --> 00:30:28,000
on where I think the larger surface area is on the top of

502
00:30:28,100 --> 00:30:30,400
the stone called the table.

503
00:30:30,500 --> 00:30:38,440
I'm going to set it in cement like that, find the, where the

504
00:30:38,540 --> 00:30:44,060
grain is on the stone and put it down on the wheel.

505
00:30:44,160 --> 00:30:48,300
The first patent on a diamond wheel was issued in 1878.

506
00:30:48,400 --> 00:30:51,800
The wheel then looked like it looks today.

507
00:30:51,801 --> 00:30:55,540
To cut a facet requires something as hard as the diamond itself.

508
00:30:55,541 --> 00:30:58,000
Master cutters use diamond powder.

509
00:30:58,001 --> 00:31:01,140
This is the loose diamond powder.

510
00:31:01,141 --> 00:31:04,880
I'll take the diamond powder and either apply it with some glue

511
00:31:04,881 --> 00:31:09,040
or some linseed oil and work it in with another diamond.

512
00:31:09,041 --> 00:31:14,080
So one part of the wheel will cut the facet down here.

513
00:31:14,081 --> 00:31:15,860
When the diamond is finished cutting,

514
00:31:15,861 --> 00:31:18,560
when I'm done with the facet, I want

515
00:31:18,561 --> 00:31:21,140
to take off the rough lines off of the surface

516
00:31:21,141 --> 00:31:24,200
to get a good polish, as you might see on a certificate,

517
00:31:24,440 --> 00:31:26,380
and have the certificate read very good.

518
00:31:26,880 --> 00:31:28,700
And then I'll start to polish it up here.

519
00:31:28,780 --> 00:31:31,720
This is a much finer powder on the top of the wheel.

520
00:31:32,400 --> 00:31:34,440
And that'll polish out the facet.

521
00:31:37,560 --> 00:31:39,420
Diamonds, like wood, have grains.

522
00:31:40,240 --> 00:31:42,260
Because diamond is a fairly clear material,

523
00:31:42,261 --> 00:31:44,580
it isn't always obvious where the grain is.

524
00:31:45,060 --> 00:31:48,160
It's a harder surface, natural surface there is.

525
00:31:48,161 --> 00:31:55,720
So if it doesn't want to cut, you could be in for a difficult day.

526
00:31:56,640 --> 00:32:00,260
While most other stages of the diamond industry have changed with technology,

527
00:32:00,760 --> 00:32:03,340
the art of cutting remains in the master's hands.

528
00:32:03,740 --> 00:32:07,980
I'm sure the day will come when computers will be able to do it all,

529
00:32:08,080 --> 00:32:12,420
and they won't need me, but I think I'll be long gone by then.

530
00:32:12,421 --> 00:32:17,920
Down the street from the Manhattan offices of Lewis

531
00:32:18,020 --> 00:32:20,720
Glick and Company is the Diamond District.

532
00:32:20,820 --> 00:32:23,060
High above the stores on West 47th Street

533
00:32:23,160 --> 00:32:27,460
is a small dimly lit fortress known as the European Gem Lab.

534
00:32:27,560 --> 00:32:30,080
Here, gemologists put cut and polished diamonds

535
00:32:30,180 --> 00:32:31,780
through a series of tests.

536
00:32:31,880 --> 00:32:35,280
In the end, each stone is given a certificate of authenticity

537
00:32:35,380 --> 00:32:37,980
that includes the stone's unique characteristics.

538
00:32:38,080 --> 00:32:40,120
In a professional gemological laboratory,

539
00:32:40,121 --> 00:32:46,480
we use zoom binocular magnification to locate external and internal characteristics that

540
00:32:46,481 --> 00:32:48,567
determine the clarity grade of a diamond.

541
00:32:48,568 --> 00:32:50,960
Identifying characteristics that can't be found

542
00:32:50,961 --> 00:32:53,507
under 10-power magnification are disregarded.

543
00:32:53,508 --> 00:32:56,620
Diamonds without inclusions or external characteristics

544
00:32:56,621 --> 00:33:02,820
under 10-power magnification can be graded as flawless, and they are very, very rare.

545
00:33:02,821 --> 00:33:14,920
Color grading involves comparing an unknown diamond to a set of master color stones that have been previously graded against an international standard.

546
00:33:15,620 --> 00:33:17,094
Next is the black box.

547
00:33:17,095 --> 00:33:21,920
Here, gemologists grade the fluorescent strength and color of a diamond.

548
00:33:23,040 --> 00:33:29,080
This scientific machine is the sarin dimension proportion analyzer, another way to identify the diamond.

549
00:33:29,081 --> 00:33:34,760
The machine works by showing a shadow image of the diamond on the computer screen,

550
00:33:35,180 --> 00:33:40,000
and the computer is analyzing the proportions from hundreds of different angles.

551
00:33:40,260 --> 00:33:45,340
That analysis is interpreted and outputted to our central database,

552
00:33:45,780 --> 00:33:51,420
which then puts that information onto the EGL USA diamond certificate.

553
00:33:52,040 --> 00:33:54,780
The relationship of the angles, the quality of the polish,

554
00:33:54,781 --> 00:33:58,120
the proportion between the top of the diamond

555
00:33:58,220 --> 00:34:00,520
and the bottom of the diamond, the size of the table

556
00:34:00,620 --> 00:34:03,020
compared to the width, and many other factors

557
00:34:03,120 --> 00:34:06,320
go integrating the quality of the cut.

558
00:34:06,420 --> 00:34:09,920
Of the four C's, carat weight, clarity grade, color grade,

559
00:34:10,020 --> 00:34:12,440
and cutting quality, how the diamond is cut

560
00:34:12,540 --> 00:34:15,340
can add the most value to the diamond.

561
00:34:15,440 --> 00:34:18,240
There's a relatively new way to identify a diamond that

562
00:34:18,340 --> 00:34:21,540
involves inscription.

563
00:34:21,640 --> 00:34:24,780
What we're looking at right now is a technician

564
00:34:24,781 --> 00:34:30,420
typing in certain characters that will be inscribed with a special laser on the girdle,

565
00:34:30,680 --> 00:34:34,580
the thin edge of a diamond. The letters are so minute,

566
00:34:34,880 --> 00:34:38,860
they cannot be seen with the naked eye. The machine is capable of inscribing just

567
00:34:38,861 --> 00:34:39,651
about anything.

568
00:34:39,652 --> 00:34:43,340
As a matter of fact, we can accurately reproduce logos, company logos,

569
00:34:43,520 --> 00:34:47,060
corporate logos, personal insignias, family crests if you want.

570
00:34:48,140 --> 00:34:52,000
For security purposes, some merchants request social security numbers.

571
00:34:52,640 --> 00:34:54,380
Lovers request terms of endearment.

572
00:34:54,720 --> 00:34:58,220
The laser itself operates in the ultraviolet region of the spectrum,

573
00:34:58,380 --> 00:35:00,920
which means there's no burning taking place of the diamond.

574
00:35:01,040 --> 00:35:02,460
It's perfectly safe for the diamond.

575
00:35:02,740 --> 00:35:04,340
We can engrave a human hair with this.

576
00:35:05,640 --> 00:35:09,360
We chose to inscribe this 5-carat diamond with our own name.

577
00:35:10,080 --> 00:35:13,240
A diamond like this would retail for around $93,000.

578
00:35:14,060 --> 00:35:16,620
But what if this diamond had been made by a machine?

579
00:35:16,621 --> 00:35:19,120
Ever since Smithson Tennant unlocked the secret

580
00:35:19,220 --> 00:35:21,620
that diamond is carbon, scientists have tried

581
00:35:21,720 --> 00:35:24,120
to make a diamond out of graphite.

582
00:35:24,220 --> 00:35:28,060
It took 150 years before they got the job done.

583
00:35:28,160 --> 00:35:31,460
To make diamonds, scientists in the laboratory

584
00:35:31,560 --> 00:35:34,580
have to mimic the deep earth.

585
00:35:34,680 --> 00:35:36,880
You have to get very high temperatures.

586
00:35:36,980 --> 00:35:38,080
And that's not so difficult.

587
00:35:38,180 --> 00:35:40,180
We know how to make high temperatures with fire

588
00:35:40,280 --> 00:35:42,520
or with electricity passing through material.

589
00:35:42,620 --> 00:35:44,680
You can get thousands of degrees of carbon

590
00:35:44,681 --> 00:35:47,680
You can get thousands of degrees, fairly straightforward.

591
00:35:47,681 --> 00:35:50,480
But you also have to get high pressure.

592
00:35:50,481 --> 00:35:55,480
Pressure is equivalent to a 100-mile column of rock.

593
00:35:55,481 --> 00:35:58,480
Henri Moisson, a French chemist who in 1884

594
00:35:58,481 --> 00:36:02,480
took up the study of fluorine, turned his attention in 1892

595
00:36:02,481 --> 00:36:06,480
to the development of the electric arc furnace.

596
00:36:06,481 --> 00:36:08,680
In an experiment involving carbon,

597
00:36:08,681 --> 00:36:12,480
Moisson claimed in 1893 to have synthesized diamonds

598
00:36:12,481 --> 00:36:14,280
in his furnace.

599
00:36:14,281 --> 00:36:16,980
He went on to win the Nobel Prize.

600
00:36:16,981 --> 00:36:18,720
At the end of runs, he would find

601
00:36:18,721 --> 00:36:22,120
that he had these glittering, clear, faceted crystals

602
00:36:22,121 --> 00:36:24,620
that he had produced in his material.

603
00:36:24,621 --> 00:36:26,720
And it turns out those faceted crystals

604
00:36:26,721 --> 00:36:29,240
looked a lot like diamonds.

605
00:36:29,241 --> 00:36:32,240
So Moisson said, I've made diamonds.

606
00:36:32,241 --> 00:36:34,640
And the world, because he was a Nobel Prize winner,

607
00:36:34,641 --> 00:36:36,240
believed him.

608
00:36:36,241 --> 00:36:38,980
Moisson actually produced silicon carbide,

609
00:36:38,981 --> 00:36:41,540
now renamed Moissonite.

610
00:36:41,541 --> 00:36:44,540
Today, moissanite is marketed as a synthetic gemstone

611
00:36:44,541 --> 00:36:46,540
because it looks so much like a diamond,

612
00:36:46,541 --> 00:36:48,540
but sells for one-tenth a price.

613
00:36:51,540 --> 00:36:55,540
In 1913, William and Lawrence Bragg, a father-son team from

614
00:36:55,541 --> 00:36:59,540
England, used the new technology of X-ray crystallography to

615
00:36:59,541 --> 00:37:01,540
determine diamonds' atomic structure.

616
00:37:03,540 --> 00:37:06,540
Building upon that knowledge, a Harvard physics professor

617
00:37:06,541 --> 00:37:09,540
began to take every material he could get his hands on,

618
00:37:09,541 --> 00:37:13,780
from water to wax and put it under tremendous pressure.

619
00:37:13,880 --> 00:37:17,480
He would take alcohols and he would take gasoline.

620
00:37:17,580 --> 00:37:19,780
And again, you'd squeeze it and you'd make all sorts of new

621
00:37:19,880 --> 00:37:20,980
materials.

622
00:37:21,080 --> 00:37:22,180
That's what Percy Bridgman did.

623
00:37:22,280 --> 00:37:23,480
He did it better than anybody.

624
00:37:23,580 --> 00:37:27,300
And for four decades of research, he did this.

625
00:37:27,400 --> 00:37:29,800
Percy Bridgman never made diamonds,

626
00:37:29,900 --> 00:37:32,200
but he pointed the way for others.

627
00:37:32,300 --> 00:37:36,040
After World War II, the can-do generation that had built the bomb

628
00:37:36,140 --> 00:37:38,640
decided to put together a team at General Electric

629
00:37:38,641 --> 00:37:41,580
and figure out how to make synthetic diamonds once and for all.

630
00:37:42,240 --> 00:37:44,720
It was called Project Super Pressure.

631
00:37:44,980 --> 00:37:48,440
The General Electric team featured four lead scientists.

632
00:37:49,720 --> 00:37:53,640
And of those four, the most outspoken, perhaps the most brilliant,

633
00:37:54,160 --> 00:37:57,500
and certainly the most bitter is Tracy Hall.

634
00:37:58,260 --> 00:38:00,840
Tracy Hall didn't share his teammates' theory

635
00:38:00,841 --> 00:38:02,920
on how to make the best high-pressure device.

636
00:38:03,440 --> 00:38:06,580
The team wanted to use steel. Hall wanted carbide.

637
00:38:06,581 --> 00:38:10,180
Tracy Hall favored a more tapered design,

638
00:38:10,181 --> 00:38:14,000
a design called the belt, in which there was a donut-like apparatus

639
00:38:14,001 --> 00:38:17,640
which held the sample in place, and then two pistons

640
00:38:17,641 --> 00:38:20,100
which squeezed together on that sample.

641
00:38:21,040 --> 00:38:25,120
Of the team members, Herb Strong was Tracy Hall's greatest rival.

642
00:38:25,740 --> 00:38:28,660
In December of 1954, Strong made a run

643
00:38:28,661 --> 00:38:30,800
and claimed to have produced two small diamonds.

644
00:38:31,760 --> 00:38:35,060
When they were reanalyzed, it was found that the little fragments

645
00:38:35,061 --> 00:38:36,620
weren't man-made diamonds.

646
00:38:36,621 --> 00:38:40,160
They were tiny mine diamonds that had slipped into the run.

647
00:38:41,720 --> 00:38:47,000
Later that December, Tracy Hall, using his belt apparatus device and similar chemical principles

648
00:38:47,001 --> 00:38:50,145
that Herb Strong and others had developed, did his run.

649
00:38:50,146 --> 00:38:52,260
And there are countless tiny diamonds

650
00:38:52,261 --> 00:38:54,633
studded in his experimental run.

651
00:38:54,634 --> 00:38:59,380
And that indeed was the first time that humans had made diamonds

652
00:38:59,381 --> 00:39:02,780
in a way that was reproducible. For their effort,

653
00:39:02,781 --> 00:39:06,160
GE gave each of the lead scientists, including Tracy Hall,

654
00:39:06,580 --> 00:39:08,460
a $25 savings bond.

655
00:39:09,400 --> 00:39:12,400
The first synthetic diamonds were like little grains of sand.

656
00:39:12,780 --> 00:39:15,860
To make big diamonds, the scientists used a different strategy.

657
00:39:16,200 --> 00:39:19,260
If you begin your experimental run with a tiny crystal,

658
00:39:19,420 --> 00:39:20,300
a tiny diamond crystal,

659
00:39:20,800 --> 00:39:23,640
then that provides a place for other carbon atoms to attach.

660
00:39:23,700 --> 00:39:26,180
And if they attach slowly and methodically,

661
00:39:26,380 --> 00:39:28,640
the small crystal grows into a large crystal.

662
00:39:29,360 --> 00:39:32,760
GE discovered you can turn almost any carbon-based substance

663
00:39:32,780 --> 00:39:35,180
into diamonds, including peanut butter.

664
00:39:35,280 --> 00:39:37,920
The idea is to create an experimental environment

665
00:39:38,020 --> 00:39:40,280
of high temperature and high pressure.

666
00:39:40,380 --> 00:39:43,320
You have a sample that's composed primarily of carbon,

667
00:39:43,420 --> 00:39:47,560
and you want to take that sample and confine it very carefully

668
00:39:47,660 --> 00:39:50,740
so that it doesn't squirt out the sides of your apparatus.

669
00:39:50,840 --> 00:39:54,540
And you want to squeeze down on that sample using pieces of steel

670
00:39:54,640 --> 00:39:58,040
or carbide or even other diamonds in some cases.

671
00:39:58,140 --> 00:40:00,540
And then you want to apply tremendous pressure

672
00:40:00,541 --> 00:40:04,980
using a large press, and these presses can be 10 or 12 feet tall,

673
00:40:05,180 --> 00:40:09,140
with thousands of tons of force applied to a very small area.

674
00:40:09,720 --> 00:40:12,580
The final step, then, is to turn on the electric juice

675
00:40:12,581 --> 00:40:14,800
and put current through the sample,

676
00:40:15,020 --> 00:40:18,220
which heats it up to extremely high temperature while it's at pressure.

677
00:40:18,900 --> 00:40:22,740
Electric current passes through two of the opposed anvils and the sample.

678
00:40:23,500 --> 00:40:26,360
In a few minutes, the pressure and temperature work their miracle.

679
00:40:27,500 --> 00:40:30,300
The cube, now smaller and gray from the heat,

680
00:40:30,540 --> 00:40:34,640
is smashed apart by a hammer. This black diamond can be used to

681
00:40:34,641 --> 00:40:38,940
attach to a drill bit that can break up solid rock.

682
00:40:38,941 --> 00:40:42,840
Early on, all synthetic diamonds were earmarked for industry.

683
00:40:42,841 --> 00:40:47,040
Now, perfect gem-quality diamonds are man-made in the laboratory,

684
00:40:47,041 --> 00:40:50,280
and they are for sale. A one-carat synthetic diamond

685
00:40:50,281 --> 00:40:53,340
sells for $600. A comparable mine diamond

686
00:40:53,341 --> 00:40:57,440
sells for $5,500. What other surprises do diamonds

687
00:40:57,441 --> 00:41:00,100
for us. Dr. Roy Lewis, a senior scientist

688
00:41:00,101 --> 00:41:03,100
at the University of Chicago who spent his career measuring the

689
00:41:03,101 --> 00:41:06,780
noble gas xenon of meteorites, stumbled upon diamonds through

690
00:41:06,781 --> 00:41:09,100
the back door. The meteorites that they're

691
00:41:09,101 --> 00:41:13,600
found in are as old as the Earth, but the diamonds came from stars

692
00:41:13,601 --> 00:41:16,940
before our solar system formed. So they're older than the Earth.

693
00:41:16,941 --> 00:41:19,440
They're diamonds. They ought to be worth something.

694
00:41:19,441 --> 00:41:21,940
If I could get a penny apiece for them, I could retire the

695
00:41:21,941 --> 00:41:25,440
national debt. Lewis keeps his pre-solar

696
00:41:25,441 --> 00:41:28,080
pre-solar diamond grains in a vial.

697
00:41:28,081 --> 00:41:30,200
He says he has 50 quadrillion.

698
00:41:30,201 --> 00:41:33,840
That's five followed by 16 zeros.

699
00:41:33,841 --> 00:41:37,400
These diamonds that I get are formed in the atmospheres

700
00:41:37,401 --> 00:41:38,380
of stars.

701
00:41:38,381 --> 00:41:40,500
So this is a fairly high temperature,

702
00:41:40,501 --> 00:41:42,300
but actually quite low pressure.

703
00:41:42,301 --> 00:41:45,840
They're carbon atoms, just like the diamonds in the Earth.

704
00:41:45,841 --> 00:41:48,880
And they are in the same arrangement,

705
00:41:48,881 --> 00:41:51,440
one with respect to the other.

706
00:41:51,441 --> 00:41:53,980
These so-called nano diamonds are so tiny,

707
00:41:53,981 --> 00:41:55,860
it is impossible to measure them individually.

708
00:41:56,440 --> 00:41:59,400
The diamonds are all found in the dark gray matter

709
00:41:59,401 --> 00:42:02,320
that's between every single piece that you can see on this thing.

710
00:42:02,820 --> 00:42:06,180
But if you throw this thing in a big bucket of hydrofluoric acid,

711
00:42:06,620 --> 00:42:07,700
most of it dissolves.

712
00:42:07,701 --> 00:42:10,920
If you do a size separation, you end up with diamonds.

713
00:42:11,960 --> 00:42:14,460
This photograph of the aggregate space diamonds

714
00:42:14,461 --> 00:42:16,080
looks like a sea of black.

715
00:42:16,500 --> 00:42:18,240
Each black speck is one diamond.

716
00:42:18,760 --> 00:42:21,080
I'm probably shedding diamonds as we speak

717
00:42:21,081 --> 00:42:24,320
Because there are so many of these diamonds,

718
00:42:24,420 --> 00:42:27,220
and they're so small, I am certainly contaminated

719
00:42:27,320 --> 00:42:28,920
with these diamonds.

720
00:42:29,020 --> 00:42:32,920
Now that's a condition few would complain about.

721
00:42:33,020 --> 00:42:35,920
It's no wonder diamonds are so prized,

722
00:42:36,020 --> 00:42:37,120
given the journey they make.

723
00:42:40,140 --> 00:42:43,240
Blasted from deep inside the earth,

724
00:42:43,340 --> 00:42:47,880
sifted from the sand, or vacuumed off the ocean's bed.

725
00:42:47,881 --> 00:42:52,280
These rough stones are sorted and sawed, ground and polished

726
00:42:52,380 --> 00:42:56,680
to fiery perfection. They're at once an indispensable

727
00:42:56,780 --> 00:43:01,120
tool of industry and the world's most coveted of jewels.

728
00:43:01,220 --> 00:43:05,540
They will always fascinate us.

729
00:43:05,640 --> 00:43:07,040
Diamonds.

